  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist. I haven't opened the dashboard to check how the panels render, and the rule hasn't had a run yet, so I haven't seen it fire.\n\n**What the data holds:** the data view has request records with these main fields:\n- `timestamp`, the time field\n- `response`, the HTTP status code, stored as a string\n- `bytes`\n- `extension`\n- `geo.src` and `geo.dest`\n- `clientip`\n- `machine.os`\n- `host`\n- `url`\n\n**Dashboard:** \"EFF-71a630a3 Web logs overview\" has four panels and defaults to the last 7 days. It is at http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-71a630a3-web-logs-overview-187bd5116d15\n- Total requests, as a single number\n- Requests over time, as an area chart\n- Responses by status code, as a pie chart\n- Average bytes by file extension, as a bar chart\n\n**Alert rule:** \"EFF-71a630a3 5xx spike\" is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`.\n- **Schedule:** it checks every minute.\n- **Condition:** it counts documents from the last 5 minutes whose `response.keyword` matches `5xx`, and fires when there are more than 10.\n- **State:** it was created disabled, so I enabled it. It has no notification action attached, so it will only record alerts in Kibana and won't notify anyone.\n\nThe sample logs are historical, so the rule won't fire unless fresh 5xx documents arrive.",
